<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/undated">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Versailles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> was the peace treaty signed in 1919 between the Allied powers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It imposed territorial losses, military restrictions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> on Germany, stripped it of its colonies, and included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-guilt-clause">War Guilt Clause</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> treat it as the final settlement of the empire's defeat, whose resentments shaped the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles.jpg" alt="Treaty of Versailles" width="276" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Lloyd George, Woodrow Wilson and Georges Clemenceau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Versailles,_English_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
